\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.1}{\ignorespaces QLIKE vs.\ MSE penalty as a function of the forecast ratio $h_t / \sigma ^2_t$. Both losses are minimized at the perfect forecast ($h_t = \sigma ^2_t$, ratio $= 1$). MSE penalizes over- and under-prediction symmetrically. QLIKE penalizes under-prediction (ratio $< 1$) much more harshly than over-prediction (ratio $> 1$), matching the asymmetric risk preferences in volatility forecasting: underestimating vol means holding too much risk.}}{280}{figure.17.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:qlike-vs-mse}{{17.1}{280}{QLIKE vs.\ MSE penalty as a function of the forecast ratio $h_t / \sigma ^2_t$. Both losses are minimized at the perfect forecast ($h_t = \sigma ^2_t$, ratio $= 1$). MSE penalizes over- and under-prediction symmetrically. QLIKE penalizes under-prediction (ratio $< 1$) much more harshly than over-prediction (ratio $> 1$), matching the asymmetric risk preferences in volatility forecasting: underestimating vol means holding too much risk}{figure.17.1}{}}
